# No end-of-line translation, anywhere, ever.
#
# This repository is byte-attested: blackbox manifests pin sha256 digests of tracked files, the
# two-host differential takes an external anchor over its manifest's bytes, and the digest sweep
# verifies rows against `git show :<path>`. On a checkout with core.autocrlf=true (the
# github-hosted windows-2022 runner's default), every text file materializes with \r\n while the
# index holds \n — so the worktree no longer matches what the repository attests, and the
# two-host differential failed its own anchor check on bytes git rewrote (#108, first matrix
# run, reproduced locally by CRLF-ifying the manifest).
#
# `-text` disables translation for every path: what is committed is what is checked out, on
# every platform. Editors on Windows handle \n fine; digests cannot handle \r\n.
* -text

# Merge driver: union for genuinely append-only docs.
#
# The merge train (scripts/merge-train.mjs) rolls N feature branches off origin/main in one
# `--no-ff` chain. When every branch appends a top entry to the same file, the conventional
# 3-way text merge produces a textual conflict even though no two branches edited the same
# line — because git's 3-way merge has no notion of "two disjoint appends to the same file
# are not a conflict". That cost the 20260818 train 28 of 31 candidates on CHANGELOG.md /
# HANDOFF.md conflicts even though the branches' CODE diffs were disjoint.
#
# `merge=union` resolves that case: every distinct line from every parent appears in the
# merge result. The trade-off is that union can:
#   1. duplicate a line that two parents edited IDENTICALLY (harmless — textually equal)
#   2. duplicate a line that two parents edited to two DIFFERENT new values (corrupting —
#      the file now carries two inconsistent versions of the same line)
#   3. misorder lines when two parents insert at the same anchor (acceptable for ledgers
#      whose readers do not depend on order)
#
# We therefore mark ONLY files where every concurrent edit is a pure append to a disjoint
# region (top of CHANGELOG.md under `## [Unreleased]`). Files where multiple branches
# MUTATE an existing line (HANDOFF.md's `**current suite contract:** NNN/NNN`, COMPATIBILITY.md
# table rows, GOAL-PROMPT.md prose sections) deliberately do NOT get `merge=union` — the
# train will still hit conflicts there, and the leftovers with reasons are the honest
# outcome. Reclassifying later requires a real audit of the new conflicts, not a blanket flip.
#
# Honest classification as of t_fca64917 (merge-train v2):
#   CHANGELOG.md    UNION-SAFE   26/28 leftovers insert a new `## [Unreleased]` block at line 3.
#   HANDOFF.md      UNION-UNSAFE 5/28 leftovers all mutate line 4 (suite count). Union would
#                                Frankenstein 5 versions of one line — actively wrong.
#   COMPATIBILITY.md UNION-UNSAFE feat/depth-numeric-gates-20260818 edits one table row in
#                                place. Union duplicates the row.
#   GOAL-PROMPT.md  UNION-UNSAFE structured prose; only one pending branch (low payoff).
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
